<script lang="ts">
  import { cities } from './parking/cities';
  import type { ScrollyStep } from './parking/types';
  import SquaresFigure from './parking/SquaresFigure.svelte';
  import ScrollyRanking from './parking/ScrollyRanking.svelte';
  import MapPanel from './parking/MapPanel.svelte';
  import TreesAreCool from './parking/TreesAreCool.svelte';
  // Vite resolves and fingerprints the file; the same illustration is the
  // post's featuredImage, where posts.ts resolves it the same way.
  import heroImage from './media/parking-to-park.jpg?url';
  import aerialImage from './media/parking-to-park-areal.jpg?url';
  // This post's display face, loaded only with this post — the rest of the site
  // keeps Bowlby One. @font-face blocks carry unicode-range per subset, so the
  // browser fetches the latin file only. To make it the site's face instead,
  // move this import and the --display override below into +layout.svelte.
  import '@fontsource/yeseva-one/400.css';

  /**
   * The two scroll-driven sequences. The sentences live here rather than in
   * <ScrollyRanking /> because they are the article's prose; the component only
   * knows how to pin them and what sort each one claims. `<b>` carries the
   * emphasis; `.g` / `.c` tint a figure in the colour that figure already means
   * elsewhere in the piece, so no highlight rests on colour alone.
   */
  const nowSteps: ScrollyStep[] = [
    {
      sortKey: 'green',
      sortDir: 'desc',
      html:
        'Turns out that the availability of green spaces available a short walk from where ' +
        'most people in a city live is the best in <b>Helsinki, Prague and Stockholm</b>. ' +
        'Here also the amount of people living close to no green is quite low.'
    },
    {
      sortKey: 'zero',
      // worst at the top, so the eight cities the sentence counts are the ones
      // the ink rule marks
      sortDir: 'desc',
      highlight: (c) => c.now.zeroGreen > 50,
      html:
        'In <b>eight cities</b> <b>more than half of the population</b> lives without any ' +
        'green close by.'
    },
    {
      sortKey: 'car',
      sortDir: 'desc',
      html:
        'This while the car infrastructure gets a steady median value of ' +
        '<b class="c">9 to 16%</b> in each of the cities.'
    }
  ];

  const convertedSteps: ScrollyStep[] = [
    {
      sortKey: 'green',
      sortDir: 'desc',
      html:
        'The median share of green in a city rises from <b class="g">0-15%</b> to ' +
        '<b class="g">6-22%</b> and <b>almost no one lives far from trees</b>.'
    },
    {
      sortKey: 'car',
      sortDir: 'desc',
      html:
        'The cars are still present, but now occupy just <b class="c">5-8%</b> of the ' +
        'median surface.'
    }
  ];
</script>

<!-- The prose quotes no live figures any more — every number it names is fixed
     in the text. `./parking/cities.ts` still exports `figures` (nCities,
     medGreen, carWins, flipCount and the converted equivalents, all derived
     from the same rows the rankings read) if a rewrite wants them back. -->

<article class="pp">
  <header class="hero" style:background-image="url({heroImage})">
    <div class="hero-inner">
      <h1>What if we got rid of cars?</h1>
      <p class="stand">
        It's getting hot in here and not in the good way.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="intro">
    <p>
      During the 2026 June heatwave, more than 16,000 people in the EU have died
      <a href="https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/europes-heat-waves-are-killing-thousands-and-wringing-its-soils-dry/">because
      of heat stress</a>. In cities, the situation is particularly dire. Any city
      is several degrees hotter than the surrounding countryside, an effect known
      as the <em>urban heat island</em>. Asphalt traps heat from the sun and keeps
      on releasing it deep into the night, giving no relief.
    </p>
    <p>
      At the end of this heatwave, I have spent a night in my hometown Bratislava
      to visit my grandmother. She lives on the fifth floor of an apartment
      building in Petržalka, the concrete jungle I grew up in. It was 'only' 34
      degrees that day, but the heat was oppressing. In the evening even with all
      windows open, the heat would not leave the flat. I did not sleep that night.
    </p>
    <p>
     I have come across multiple articles about how Europe should embrace air conditioning,
     but to me that seems to be symptom relief rather than grabbing the problem by the roots.
    The real issue being in how we design our cities, the materials we build with, and how we choose to allocate urban space.
    In this essay, I explore what might happen if we replaced car infrastructure with trees.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="why">
    <h2>Trees are cool</h2>
    <p>
      Any surface can bounce energy back to the sky, store it, heat the air with
      it, or spend it evaporating water. Asphalt cannot evaporate water, so it
      heats the air and absorbs a lot of the energy and releases the heat later. That is how an urban heat island emerges.
    </p>
    <p>
      Asphalt's own surface can swing between roughly 40°C and 60°C on a sunny
      day, and it keeps releasing that stored heat into the air well after
      sunset. This is what makes cities suffocating in a heatwave.
    </p>
    <p>
      Trees, on the other hand, <a href="https://theconversation.com/can-trees-really-cool-our-cities-down-44099">cool
      the city down</a>. Their shade prevents asphalt from absorbing the heat in
      the first place, and they use the energy they absorb to evaporate water in
      a process called <em>evapotranspiration</em>. It is what it sounds like: trees sweating! 
      Just evaporating water like this can roughly <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-71825-x">halve the
      urban heat island effect</a>.
    </p>
      <TreesAreCool />
  </section>

  <section class="why">
    <h2>It's not that crazy</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lancet/article/PIIS0140-6736(22)02585-5/fulltext">Raising
      tree coverage</a> could prevent deaths. Yet Europe is short of the green
      that does the cooling. This year scientists
      <a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-026-71523-8">looked at 862
      European cities</a> to see how many adhere to the 3-30-300 rule: 3 trees
      visible from every home, 30% neighborhood canopy cover, and a park within
      300 meters. They found that 15% of the population live in areas
      that meet <b>all</b> three criteria, while even more (21%) live in areas that meet <b>none</b> of
      them.
    </p>
    <p>
      While ground in cities is scarce and valuable, there is one type of
      infrastructure we do allocate a lot of space to: cars.
      So what if we removed some
      of that car infrastructure?
    </p>

    <figure class="aerial">
      <img
        src={aerialImage}
        alt="Two aerial sketches of the same few blocks. On the left, wide roads and
             rows of surface parking between the buildings; on the right, the same
             streets and lots drawn over with trees, lawns and paths, the river
             edged in green."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
    </figure>

    <p>
      Turns out, good things happen. Pontevedra, in western Spain,
      <a href="https://www.politico.eu/article/pontevedra-city-pioneer-europe-car-free-future/">banned
      most cars</a> from its center in 1999. Traffic fell 53%, air pollution 67%,
      and the last traffic death was 15 years ago. Oslo
      <a href="https://www.fastcompany.com/90294948/what-happened-when-oslo-decided-to-make-its-downtown-basically-car-free">cleared
      cars</a> from its downtown core and turned the parking into benches, bike
      lanes and pocket parks. Paris set to remove 70,000 on-street parking spots
      and <a href="https://www.leparisien.fr/info-paris-ile-de-france-oise/transports/stationnement-a-paris-24-000-places-supprimees-en-six-ans-loin-des-70-000-promises-07-02-2026-ET2CCP54MBE2HE6343TMW2YG3E.php">24,000
      were gone</a> by early 2026. Barcelona's superblock plan aims to
      <a href="https://kleinmanenergy.upenn.edu/research/publications/superblocks-barcelonas-plan-to-free-itself-from-cars/">reclaim
      around 60%–70% of the street space</a> currently given over to cars,
      converting interior streets into space for pedestrians, bicycles and trees.
    </p>
    <p>
      In fact, cities they work better without cars. Time and time again when a city has closed off streets
      or removed parking spots, instead of increased congestion, the
      <a href="https://www.researchgate.net/publication/248177303_Traffic_Evaporation_-_What_Really_Happens_When_Road_Space_Is_Reallocated">cars
      simply vanish</a>. And trees, they are good for us. Being around trees has been linked to a 
      range of health benefits, including lower blood pressure, less stress, a slower heart rate and lower rates of diabetes.
      It's an act of wellness and the Japanese have the word for it: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shinrin-yoku">森林浴</a> (森林 = forest, 浴 = bath).
    </p>
    <p>
     So what would happen if we sacrificed cars to trees?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="board">
    <div class="board-head">
      <h2>Parks vs parking</h2>
      <p class="body-copy">
        For this little experiment, I have cut every city into 500&nbsp;m squares.
        500 meters is a five to ten minute walk, depends on how fast you go. In
        each square I measured how much of it is car infrastructure (road and
        parking) and how much is <em>urban green</em>, spaces you can enter and
        use for relief from heat: parks, gardens, cemeteries, forests, woods and
        nature reserves, private and public of at least 0.5&nbsp;ha (see also the
        methodology below). 
      </p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        For example: Prague's urban core holds 1,052 of these
        squares, and its median resident's square is <b class="sqg">14% green</b>
        and <b class="sqc">11% car</b>. 8% of its residents live in a square like
        <b>C</b>, which holds none.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="squares">
      <div class="legend">
        <span><i style="background:#0a6b40"></i>Urban green</span>
        <span><i style="background:#7d4a9e"></i>Car infrastructure</span>
        <span><i style="background:#c3d9e8"></i>Water</span>
      </div>
      <SquaresFigure />
    </div>
    <p class="body-copy">
      To find a typical (median) value for each city, I ordered all its squares on the amount of green.
      Then I let the script go up the ordered squares and count the population until it reached half of the city population.
      That square became the typical square as half of the population lives in squares with less and half with more green.
      So instead of giving every square the same importance, I gave more weight to squares where more people live.
      This way, a big forest on the edge of the city doesn't matter as much as a small park that many people can walk to.
    </p>
  </section>

  <ScrollyRanking
    rows={cities}
    steps={nowSteps}
    label="The 39 capitals as measured, ranked"
  />

  <section class="why">
    <p>
      So let's run an experiment. What would happen if we replaced the surface
      parking and 60% of the small streets in a city with green?
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <strong>NOW and CONVERTED stay live</strong> while you scroll — switch between the
      cities as they were measured and the same cities with their parking planted.
    </p>
  </section>

  <ScrollyRanking
    rows={cities}
    steps={convertedSteps}
    stateKey="scenario"
    stateToggle
    label="The 39 capitals with their parking converted to green, ranked"
  />

  <section class="why closing">
    <p>
      I am just a data analyst with a curious mind, not an urban planner. But from the numerous examples of cities that have
      pushed out the cars infrastructure in favor of trees, it is pretty obvious to me that we can do something about the suffering that heatwaves bring to our cities if we rethink what a city should look like,
      and who and what it should serve. While might need to embrace the air conditioning a bit more, but I believe we have to look also at how we build our cities and for whom: comfortable for the few or livable for everybody.
    </p>
  </section>

  <MapPanel rows={cities} />

  <section class="method">
    <h2>How this was measured</h2>
    <h3>Green and car infrastructure</h3>
    <p>
      Urban green = OpenStreetMap polygons of parks, gardens, recreation grounds, commons, village greens, dog parks and
      cemeteries, the same public recreational categories the EU's <a href="https://land.copernicus.eu/en/products/urban-atlas">Urban
      Atlas</a> calls "green urban areas", and the same OSM tags used to monitor
      <a href="https://www.mdpi.com/2073-445X/13/12/2174">UN SDG 11.7.1</a>,
      plus nature reserves, and forest and wood together. Dissolved so overlaps
      count once, open water subtracted, and nothing under 0.5&nbsp;ha (the size
      at which the
      <a href="https://www.who.int/europe/publications/i/item/WHO-EURO-2016-3352-43111-60341">WHO's
      evidence review</a> starts counting a green space at all). The categories
      verge grass, meadow, grassland, heath, scrub and gated allotments are left
      out of the calculation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Car infrastructure = every carriageway plus every surface parking area,
      dissolved together so overlaps count once. Roads are buffered from OSM
      centrelines: the tagged <code>width</code> where present, otherwise
      lanes × 3.25 m, otherwise
      <a href="https://www.cogitatiopress.com/urbanplanning/article/view/1209">defaults
      per road class</a>. Kerb-to-kerb means sidewalks and separated cycle tracks
      are excluded by construction; painted bike and bus lanes are included.
      Multi-storey and underground parking is excluded: this measures ground.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each 500&nbsp;m square that is more water than land, or that hangs off the
      core's edge, is dropped. Every square left gets its own green share and car
      share of its land, and its resident count from GHS-POP; both medians are
      population-weighted, squares sorted by value until cumulative population
      crosses half. "No green" is the share of a core's residents standing in a
      square with no green in it at all. The converted state re-runs all three
      with every surface parking lot and 60% of the calm-street area moved from
      car to green.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything is measured inside each capital's 'urban centre', according to
      the EU/UN Degree of Urbanisation definition (GHS-SMOD): the contiguous
      dense settlement, intersected with the municipal boundary, and divided by
      the residents of that same area. On the top of the map it states what share
      of the municipal population lives in the core being measured; in compact
      capitals it is nearly everyone, in sprawling ones (Vilnius, Belgrade) a
      quarter to a third live outside it.
    </p>
    <h3>Energy transformation of trees and asphalt</h3>
    <p>
      Figures are illustrative, not measurements, calculated with Claude and cross checked with the sources below.
      Both columns are an instantaneous midday snapshot. Incoming shortwave radiation of 850 W/m² represents clear-sky midday conditions in mid-latitude summer.
      Albedo values (asphalt 0.12, park 0.18, a half grass half canopy mix)
      and surface emissivities follow standard published ranges (Oke 1987; <a href="https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/urban-climates/urban-climates/0690FCE57A4E8E234524C2EE1A20C2BE">Oke, Mills, Christen & Voogt 2017</a>).
      Longwave losses are calculated from assumed surface temperatures of approximately 53 °C for asphalt and 30 °C for the park canopy.
      How much of what is left goes into the ground follows storage partition coefficients of 0.60 for asphalt and
      0.10 for the park, inside the ranges <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/atmos14121778">Hsieh, Chiu, Huang &amp; Visessri (2023)</a>
      compiled from published surface energy balances: 0.21–0.71 for urban surfaces, 0.07–0.31 for vegetation.
      The partitioning of available energy is consistent with <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1352231099001338">Anandakumar (1999)</a>
       for dry asphalt and <a href="https://rmets.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/1097-0088(200007)20:9%3C1033::AID-JOC508%3E3.0.CO;2-U">Spronken-Smith, Oke & Lowry (2000)</a>
      for an irrigated urban park. Neither study reports the values shown here. Actual fluxes vary with latitude, season, sky condition, canopy structure, irrigation regime and surrounding urban form.
      </p>
      <p>
      If you find a substantial issues with this methodology please get in touch!
    </p>

    <p class="credit">
      Geometry © OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL (incl. ocean polygons via
      osmdata.openstreetmap.de). <br>Population, land &amp; urban centres: EU JRC GHSL
      (GHS-POP R2023A, GHS-LAND R2022A, GHS-SMOD R2023A).<br>
      Urban heat island: Copernicus C3S / VITO UrbClim intensity model via the
      EEA, CC BY 4.0.<br>
      Paris road widths calibrated against Ville de Paris open data (Plan de
      Voirie). <br>Analysis and code by Ada Homolova & Claude Opus 5
    </p>
  </section>
</article>

<style>
  /* Everything here is scoped to this post's component. The three colour
     variables the piece needs (park / asphalt / parking purple) hang off .pp
     rather than :root so they cannot leak into the rest of the site; the
     typography and neutral variables (--display, --slab, --measure,
     --container, --orange, --muted, --body, --body-soft, --card, --ink)
     already exist site-wide in +layout.svelte with the same values the
     prototype used. Fonts come from @fontsource in the root layout — the
     prototype's base64-inlined @font-face blocks are gone. */
  .pp {
    --parking: #6d3f96;
    --park: #0a6b40;
    --asphalt: #7d4a9e;
    --plate: #fdf5f0;
    /* the illustration's paper colour, used for the scrim under the hero type */
    --paper: 246, 242, 234;
    /* art-nouveau display face for this post's headings only, overriding the
       site's Bowlby One. Single weight (400), so nothing here asks for bold. */
    --display: 'Yeseva One', Georgia, serif;
    max-width: 84rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2.5rem var(--gutter) 4rem;
  }

  /* The opening band: the parking-to-park illustration as a background, pulled
     out to the article container's edges (over the .pp gutter and top padding),
     with the title block centred on it. The drawing is a pale pencil sketch, so
     the type needs a paper-coloured scrim to stay readable over the foliage —
     ::before rather than a gradient on the element itself, so the image keeps
     its own colours where no text sits. */
  .hero {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    align-content: center;
    min-height: clamp(20rem, 42vw, 32rem);
    margin: -2.5rem calc(-1 * var(--gutter)) clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 6rem) var(--gutter) clamp(5rem, 13vw, 9rem);
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center 62%;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }
  /* Two stacked washes: the paper-coloured scrim that keeps the type legible,
     and above it a fade to the page background so the drawing dissolves into
     the article instead of ending on a rule. The bottom padding above keeps the
     text clear of the fade. rgb(255,255,255) is --bg, spelled out because the
     fade needs it at partial alpha. */
  .hero::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background:
      linear-gradient(
        to bottom,
        rgba(255, 255, 255, 0) 52%,
        rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55) 78%,
        rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9) 92%,
        rgb(255, 255, 255) 100%
      ),
      linear-gradient(
        to bottom,
        rgba(var(--paper), 0.42),
        rgba(var(--paper), 0.74) 52%,
        rgba(var(--paper), 0.5)
      );
  }
  .hero-inner {
    position: relative;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  /* full ink over the drawing, where --body would go soft on the foliage */
  .hero .stand { color: var(--ink); }
  /* on a phone the band is the whole first screen — the drawing needs the room
     more than the text does, and the title block rides in its lower half */
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .hero {
      align-content: end;
      min-height: 76svh;
      padding-top: clamp(6rem, 34vw, 12rem);
      background-position: 58% 58%;
    }
  }
  .pp :global(section) {
    margin-bottom: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5.5rem);
  }

  .pp :global(h1) {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.9rem);
    line-height: 1.02;
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .pp :global(h2) {
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3.2vw, 2.1rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
  }
  .pp :global(h3) {
    font-family: var(--slab);
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }

  /* every prose column is centred in the 84rem article, the way the rest of the
     site centres its reading measure — only the figures and the board go wide */
  .intro,
  .why,
  .method,
  .board-head,
  .body-copy {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
  .stand {
    font-family: var(--slab);
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 2vw, 1.25rem);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--body);
    margin: 1.1rem 0 0;
  }
  .intro :global(p) { color: var(--body); }
  /* .body-copy is what the .board section's own paragraphs wear: the section
     spans the full 84rem so the Prague squares figure can go wide, so its prose
     has to opt back in to the reading measure the way .intro / .why / .method
     get it for free */
  .body-copy { color: var(--body); }
  .note {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--body-soft);
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    margin: 1.6rem auto 1.1rem;
  }
  /* the .board section spans the full 84rem so the squares figure can go wide;
     the prose inside it keeps the reading measure via .body-copy instead. The
     two scrolly rankings and the map panel that follow set their own widths. */
  .board-head { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  /* The before/after aerial, answering the question the paragraph above it asks.
     Same treatment as the hero: no frame, no caption, the drawing just fades
     in and out of the page. Here it is a mask rather than a scrim, so the
     <img> keeps its alt text and the fade works whatever sits behind it. Held
     to the reading measure so it reads as part of the argument rather than
     interrupting it; the drawing is 1200px wide, so that never blows it up past
     native. */
  .aerial {
    margin: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 3rem) auto clamp(2rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .aerial img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(
      to bottom,
      transparent 0,
      #000 14%,
      #000 86%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, transparent 0, #000 14%, #000 86%, transparent 100%);
  }

  /* the Prague figure draws at 46rem internally, so the block is centred at
     that width rather than at the container's */
  .squares { margin: 1.6rem auto; max-width: 46rem; }

  .legend {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem 1.1rem;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    margin: 0 0 0.7rem;
  }
  .legend span {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
  }
  .legend i {
    width: 0.85rem;
    height: 0.85rem;
    border: 1.5px solid var(--ink);
    display: block;
    flex: none;
  }

  /* the two inline read-outs in the Prague paragraph, matching the figure */
  .sqg {
    color: var(--park);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .sqc {
    color: var(--asphalt);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* .why holds child components (<TreesAreCool />), so the body colour goes on
     the container and inherits down rather than on a `.why :global(p)`
     descendant selector. That selector reached through the component boundary
     and restyled the child's own paragraphs too — and at 0,2,1 it out-specified
     the component's own single-class rules, so the child could not win its own
     colours back. color inherits, so this still reaches this section's prose;
     it just stops leaking into a child's internals. Don't reintroduce
     `.why :global(p)` here. */
  .why { max-width: var(--measure); color: var(--body); }
  .pp :global(figcaption) {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .pp :global(figcaption a),
  .why :global(a),
  .method :global(a),
  .board :global(a),
  .intro :global(a) {
    border-bottom: 1.5px solid var(--orange);
  }

  .method { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .method :global(p) {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--body-soft);
    margin: 0.7rem 0 0;
  }
  /* The two subheads split the method section into its two halves. They step
     *down* in size from the quiet body text around them and *up* in weight and
     colour — slab caps in full ink — so they organise the reference matter
     without competing with the Yeseva <h2> above or the essay proper. The rule
     is the piece's recurring 1.5px ink border, doing the grouping work: the
     space is all above the rule, so each heading reads as belonging to the
     paragraphs under it rather than floating between two blocks. Ink on white
     (20:1), so nothing here rests on hue. */
  .method h3 {
    font-family: var(--slab);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    line-height: 1.35;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink);
    border-top: 1.5px solid var(--ink);
    margin: 2.6rem 0 0;
    padding-top: 0.55rem;
  }
  /* the first subhead sits directly under the <h2>, where a rule would read as
     an underline of the section title rather than a divider inside it */
  .method h2 + h3 {
    border-top: 0;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    padding-top: 0;
  }
  .method :global(code) {
    font-size: 0.85em;
    background: var(--plate);
    padding: 0 0.2em;
  }
  .method .credit {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    margin: 1.6rem 0 0;
  }
</style>
